WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.856
Priya’s bakery pays the premiums for its short-term and long-term disability plans.

00:00:04.856 --> 00:00:10.522
Those premiums are a deductible business expense and are not income to the employees.

00:00:10.522 --> 00:00:15.784
Because the employer paid, benefits are taxable to the employee who collects them.

00:00:15.784 --> 00:00:20.640
If employees pay the premium themselves with after-tax dollars, benefits are tax-free.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.687
If they pay through a cafeteria plan with pre-tax dollars,

00:00:24.687 --> 00:00:28.734
the result is the same as employer payment: taxable benefits.

00:00:28.734 --> 00:00:33.995
Where premiums are shared, benefits are taxable in proportion to the employer-paid share.

00:00:33.995 --> 00:00:37.638
Some employers gross up the premium so employees pay

00:00:37.638 --> 00:00:41.280
tax on it now and receive tax-free benefits later.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.558
Employer-paid premiums for group medical and dental plans are deductible to

00:00:46.558 --> 00:00:51.835
the employer and excluded from employees’ income, and the benefits, payments

00:00:51.835 --> 00:00:57.113
for care, are tax-free to employees under the medical expense exception.

00:00:57.113 --> 00:01:00.951
Employee contributions through a cafeteria plan are pre-tax.

00:01:00.951 --> 00:01:07.668
Employer-paid premiums for tax-qualified group long-term care insurance receive the same treatment: deductible to

00:01:07.668 --> 00:01:13.906
the employer, excluded from employee income within the normal limits, with tax-free benefits.

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:20.143
Long-term care insurance may not, however, be offered through a cafeteria plan or

00:01:20.143 --> 00:01:26.380
flexible spending account; employees who want to pay for it pay after tax.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.762
Priya adds group accidental death and dismemberment coverage.

00:01:29.762 --> 00:01:35.257
For tax purposes AD&D is treated as accident and health insurance, not life

00:01:35.257 --> 00:01:40.752
insurance, so employer-paid premiums are deductible and are not income to employees, without

00:01:40.752 --> 00:01:45.402
the 50,000 dollar coverage threshold that applies to group term life.

00:01:45.402 --> 00:01:50.052
Benefits are tax-free: a dismemberment payment to the employee and a

00:01:50.052 --> 00:01:54.702
death benefit to the beneficiary are both received without income tax.

00:01:54.702 --> 00:02:00.197
Because the premium is modest and the tax result is favorable in every

00:02:00.197 --> 00:02:05.270
direction, group AD&D is a common addition to small employer benefit packages.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.959
Rosa works for herself.

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:12.450
She cannot exclude an employer’s premium because she has no employer, but Congress

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:17.940
gave her the self-employed health insurance deduction: she may deduct 100 percent of

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:23.430
the premiums for medical and dental coverage for herself, her spouse and dependents,

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:28.920
and qualified long-term care premiums within the age-based limits, as an adjustment to

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.721
income without itemizing or meeting the 7.5 percent floor.

00:02:32.721 --> 00:02:38.634
The deduction cannot exceed her net earnings from self-employment and is not available for

00:02:38.634 --> 00:02:44.547
any month she is eligible for an employer-subsidized plan, her own or a spouse’s.

00:02:44.547 --> 00:02:48.770
Partners and more-than-2-percent S corporation shareholders use the same deduction.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:53.343
The three business policies from Lesson 33 follow the master rule.

00:02:53.343 --> 00:02:57.084
Key employee disability income: the business pays the premium,

00:02:57.084 --> 00:03:00.825
may not deduct it, and receives the benefit tax-free.

00:03:00.825 --> 00:03:05.813
Business overhead expense: the business pays and deducts the premium, and the

00:03:05.813 --> 00:03:10.801
benefit is taxable income to the business, but the overhead it reimburses,

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:15.790
rent, utilities and wages, is deductible, so the tax largely nets out.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:21.194
Disability buyout: premiums are not deductible and the benefit used to purchase the

00:03:21.194 --> 00:03:26.597
disabled owner’s interest is received tax-free; the selling owner then recognizes gain or

00:03:26.597 --> 00:03:31.170
loss on the sale of her interest under the ordinary rules.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.339
Line the cases up and the pattern holds.

00:03:34.339 --> 00:03:39.094
Employer-paid disability benefits are taxable because the premium was deducted and excluded.

00:03:39.094 --> 00:03:42.263
Employer-paid medical, dental and long-term care benefits are

00:03:42.263 --> 00:03:45.036
tax-free because Congress exempts payments for care.

00:03:45.036 --> 00:03:47.809
AD&D premiums and benefits are both favored.

00:03:47.809 --> 00:03:52.564
The self-employed deduct their premiums above the line while their benefits stay

00:03:52.564 --> 00:03:56.921
tax-free, the one place the master rule bends for medical coverage.

00:03:56.921 --> 00:04:01.279
Key person and buyout premiums are nondeductible, so their benefits are

00:04:01.279 --> 00:04:05.637
tax-free; the overhead policy deducts premiums, so its benefits are taxable.

00:04:05.637 --> 00:04:08.807
When a question asks about taxation, ask first

00:04:08.807 --> 00:04:11.580
who paid and whether it was deducted.
